.TH ENGINE1999C 1 "October 2026" "Engine 1999 @VERSION@" "Engine1999 Manual"
.SH NAME
engine1999c \- the BASIC system of Engine1999 for the command line (no window)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B engine1999c
[\fIoption\fR ...]
.RI [ file " | " \- " [" argument " ...]]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B engine1999c
is the console host of Engine1999: the same BASIC system as
.BR engine1999 (1),
without a window and without SDL, for shell scripts, pipes and servers.
.PP
With a
.I file
(a program as a text listing, \fI.bas\fR, or in the binary form, \fI.e99\fR; a name without extension is found as with
.BR LOAD )
it loads the program, runs it and ends.
Without a file, or with
.BR \- ,
it reads BASIC lines from standard input: a line with a line number is stored, any other line runs at once; on a terminal it shows a banner
and the
.B Ready.
prompt.
The input of a running program
.RB ( INPUT ", " "LINE INPUT" ", " GET ", " INKEY$ )
comes from the same standard input, in order.
.PP
A script can start with a line
.BR #!/usr/bin/env\ engine1999c ;
the loader skips a first line that begins with
.BR #! .
.SH OPTIONS
Options come before the file. Everything after the file (or after
.BR \- )
belongs to the program.
.TP
.B \-i
After running the file, go on with the lines from standard input.
.TP
.BR \-
Read the program lines from standard input (the default without a file).
.TP
.BR \-\-color [ =\fIwhen\fR ]
Syntax colors in
.B LIST
and
.BR FILES :
.BR always ,
.B never
or
.B auto
(the default: on a terminal, unless the environment variable
.B NO_COLOR
is set).
.B \-\-color
alone means
.BR always .
.TP
.B \-\-list\-keywords
Print the reserved words of the language, one per line.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help text and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-tokenize
Print the token bytes of every input line (to standard output).
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-detokenize
Print every input line rebuilt from its tokens.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-parse
Print every token as the compiler fetches it.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-tree
Print the syntax tree after parsing.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-code
Print every instruction the code generator emits.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-all
All of the debug outputs (not
.BR \-\-debug\-keys ).
.TP
.B \-\-debug\-keys
Show what the terminal sends for each key instead of running BASIC: the bytes of every key press (those arriving within 30 ms belong together) as hex and as a C string, with what they are (a cursor or function key with its modifiers, a control code, a UTF-8 character, an unknown escape sequence).
Ctrl-D or the end of the input stops it.
This is for comparing the sequences of a terminal with the ones the Engine uses.
.SH PROGRAM ARGUMENTS AND ENVIRONMENT
The words after the file are the program's: with
.B engine1999c prog.bas a "b c"
the BASIC function
.B ARGC
is 2,
.B ARG$(1)
is
.BR a ,
.B ARG$(2)
is
.B b c
and
.B ARG$(0)
is
.B prog.bas
(\fB\-\fR for standard input).
.B ENV$("NAME")
reads an environment variable.
.SH INPUT AND OUTPUT
Program output goes to standard output (binary safe), messages and errors to standard error (\fB? Division by zero in line 20\fR).
The BASIC drivers
.BR stdin: ,
.B stdout:
and
.B stderr:
open the process's streams as channels:
.B OPEN "stderr:" FOR OUTPUT AS #1.
Ctrl\-C is Break while a program runs.
The prompt and
.B INPUT
read their lines with the same one-line editor as the window (and as
.BR "EDIT n" ):
characters are typed at the cursor, Left, Right, Home, End, Backspace and Delete edit, Return accepts the line, Esc (or Ctrl\-C) cancels it (Break at INPUT), Ctrl\-D on an empty line ends the input; lines are cut at 255 characters (1023 for INPUT).
A redirected input is no special case: the bytes of the file or pipe are the keys of the same editor (CR LF counts as one line end, the end of the input completes a last line without one), and nothing is drawn.
On a terminal
.B GET
and
.B INKEY$
take single keys without echo; in a pipe they read the next byte (a line end is RETURN, 13).
Cursor and function keys arrive as ANSI escape sequences in the xterm form (\fBESC [ A\fR for Up, \fBESC [ 1 ; 5 D\fR for Ctrl+Left, \fBESC O P\fR for F1), whatever dialect the terminal sends; see
.BR engine1999\-basic (7).
On a terminal the text is ISO\-8859\-15 inside the Engine and UTF\-8 outside: typed characters are converted to it and output from code 128 up is converted back; in a pipe or a file the bytes are not changed.
.PP
Sound statements do nothing (their arguments are still checked).
.B CLS
and
.B LOCATE
write ANSI escape sequences when standard output is a terminal.
All other graphics statements fail with
.BR "? Not available in the console host" .
.B EDIT
without a target (the full-screen program editor) and
.B MONITOR
(the machine monitor) run on a terminal, with the same keys as in the window: the terminal's alternate screen is used (the shell's text comes back when they end), colors are exact with
.B COLORTERM=truecolor
(else the nearest of 256), and they follow the size of the terminal when it is resized.
A terminal smaller than 24 x 6 shows "Too small".
Without a terminal both fail with
.BR "? EDIT and MONITOR need a terminal" .
On a terminal a lone ESC breaks a running program, like Ctrl\-C (a terminal has no PAUSE/BREAK key).
A program that uses ESC itself says
.B BREAK OFF
and is then still stopped by Ctrl\-C.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program ended, or the input ended.
.TP
.I n
The program executed
.B BYE n
or
.B QUIT n
(0 to 255).
.TP
.B 1
A program run from a file ended with an error.
.TP
.B 2
The file could not be loaded, or the command line is wrong.
.SH BUILDING EXECUTABLES
.B BUILD "name"
in this host makes an executable without a window, which needs only the C and the math library
.RB ( "BUILD ""name"", WINDOW" " makes the other kind)."
It has the same input, output, arguments and exit status as described here, and takes one option of its own,
.BR \-\-engine1999\-about ,
as the first argument: it prints the license notice of the runtime.
See
.BR engine1999\-basic (7).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
engine1999c report.bas 2026 > report.txt
echo 'PRINT 2^10' | engine1999c
printf '10 INPUT A$\\n20 PRINT "hello ";A$\\nRUN\\nworld\\n' | engine1999c
engine1999c \-\-color=always prog.bas | less \-R
.fi
.PP
A script:
.PP
.nf
#!/usr/bin/env engine1999c
10 IF ARGC < 1 THEN PRINT "usage: greet NAME": BYE 2
20 PRINT "Hello, "; ARG$(1)
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Switches the syntax colors of \fB\-\-color=auto\fR off.
.TP
.BR ENGINE1999_HOME ", " ENGINE1999_LD ", " ENGINE1999_BUILD_TRACE ", " LIBRARY_PATH ", " TMPDIR
As for
.BR BUILD ;
see
.BR engine1999 (1).
